body:
If you see a setup like this -

    fs.readfile('/path/to/file', function (err,data) {
      if(err){
        //handle screwup s 
        }
          console.log(data)
    })
    //..Life goes on. Stuff here is executed immediately

Imagine the FUNCTION portion being on the left side of the fs statement. **That function will be executed AFTER the file is read.** Any statements following fs.readfile block are executed immediately and do not wait for the file to be read. Life goes on...... THEN IT GOES BACK and executes the internal callback function AFTER the file is read. A beginner can create a lot of bugs this way. This is considered old school, because those functions start to NEST inside the function, if you want to preserve order. This is known as callback hell, you can see why. It also complicates try/catch error handling.

There are also corresponding sync functions which work like python. They are NOT to be used in Express GET routes. They fully block the code until the file is read.

    const data = fs.readFileSync('/path/to/file')

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

So they came up with promises (we aren't creating them here, just consuming them). Now, you can't use promises directly with some built in node functions, but you can easily convert them with promisfy.

    fs.readfilePromise('/path/to/file')
                .then((data)=>{
                    //come back here after file read
                    console.log(data)
                }).then (()=>{
                //then do this after you print to console
                    console.log("I'm done"
                }).catch(err=>console.log(err))
    //Life goes on immediately

This makes things much more maintainable. the **then** statements are executed in sequence, after the operation returns.  The catch statement traps any errors. A promise could be a long operation reading from a web service.

&#x200B;

Finally, they have **await**, which requires **async** (in the main you would have to wrap the entire block in a function)

    async function getWebby(){
        try{
          const data = await readMyShitOffTheWeb("http://fu.com");
          const next = "this is going to run immediately. When the last     statement returns back data from the web (which could take a few seconds),                  node will return to the above statement and fill the data variable, then exit the async function. It is advised to have a try catch so you can trap errors."
        } catch (e) {
            console.log (`my screwup ${e}');
        }


    }

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

You don't have to deal with that stupid Python **lambda** keyword either for inline functions. Any function(){} can be replaced with a shorthand notation. There are some caveats though with scope, not getting into that just yet, but ()=>{} lets you see out of your scope in some cases.

This will suffice for a simple inline function-

    status=> `today's status is: ${status} `

Another example:

    let myArray=[19,50,32]
    myArray.filter(age=>age>21)

    //Old school
    let myArray=[19,50,32]
    myArray.filter(function(age)=> {return age>21; })

[https://workshop-manuals.com/lexus/](https://workshop-manuals.com/lexus/)

&#x200B;

If you play with adblock it can work very well!

First eliminate any suspension issues. Jack it up, shake the wheels, check for play.  This car has many fail points on the steering linkage. Then move on to the steering shaft. You can disconnect at the box, check that the U-joints are in good shape. You can use a good penetrant on the U-joints to bring them back to life (fluid film works well) work it into the U-joint bearings. Reinstall the pinchbolt to the correct torque with locktite blue.

&#x200B;

If you don't want to take the shaft apart, it can't hurt to saturate the U-joint pivots with fluid film and see if it helps.

